def point_in_triangle(p, a, b, c):
    def cross(ox, oy, ax, ay, bx, by):
        return (ax - ox)*(by - oy) - (ay - oy)*(bx - ox)
    d1 = cross(*p, *b, *c)
    d2 = cross(*p, *c, *a)
    d3 = cross(*p, *a, *b)
    return (d1 >= 0 and d2 >= 0 and d3 >= 0) or (d1 <= 0 and d2 <= 0 and d3 <= 0)
```

The function checks on which side of each edge the point lies. For triangle a-b-c, the cross product of p with the edge form a signed area that is positive when p lies on one side of the edge and negative on the other; the exact zero means the two lines are colinear, i.e. p lies directly on the edge. The three signs must all be non-negative, or all be non-positive. Either pattern happens exactly when p sits inside or on the triangle, so the function returns True precisely in that case.

The first argument of the outer assert, (1, 1), is inside the (0, 0), (4, 0), (0, 4) triangle; the second argument, (10, 10), is outside it; and the third could be taken as one case where an input point is also a vertex. The check would reject a vertex only if its side signs are mixed, since a vertex lies exactly on every one of the three edges.

