def replace_value(grid, old, new):
    if old == new:
        return [row[:] for row in grid]
    new_grid = []
    for row in grid:
        new_row = []
        for cell in row:
            if cell == old:
                new_row.append(new)
            else:
                new_row.append(cell)
        new_grid.append(new_row)
    return new_grid
```

The function creates a copy of the grid and replaces every cell whose value equals old with new, omits unchanged cells, and preserves the shape.

The first branch short-circuits any call where old equals new: there is no need to replace anything, so both input rows and output rows are slices of the same object. Every other call builds a complete copy, row by row. A new row starts as an empty list, and every cell is added to it as it is read: if the cell equals old, the value added is new; otherwise it is the cell unchanged. The two asserts that have multiple rows, [[1, 2], [3, 1]] and [[0, 0], [0, 0]], benefit from this row-wise copy, as do the two-cell and three-cell one-row cases.

The special branch saves work in a common class of calls, and the copied-rows return leaves the original untouched, which is all the input protection required.

