The document starts empty, and a tuple of ("do", value) adds that value to the end of it, discarding whatever had been kept in the redo history. A command of ("undo",) moves the last document value to redo, if the document is not empty; (redo,) pushes the most recently undone value from redo back onto the end, or does nothing if the redo history is already empty. An effective way to model this is to keep the document itself, then a stack called redo that holds the values removed by the most recent undo. Every "do" clears redo, and each undo and redo moves one value from one stack to the other.

