The elevator moves one floor at a time in the given direction, stops only on requested floors, and runs until it reaches the bottom of the building on a downward sweep or the topFloor on an upward sweep, with that final floor checked last.

The starting floor is always checked first: it is placed at the front of the answer list if it is in the requests list. Then the sweep proceeds by incrementing floor by the direction step until it goes out of range, adding any other requested floor that the sweep lands on. The loop ends at the first boundary floor of the right type, whether topFloor reached by going up or floor 1 reached by going down. The requested list is stored as a set for efficient membership tests, and no bounds check is needed inside the loop because the direction step takes the elevator across the limits on the next sweep.

In the first example, starting at floor 1 heading up, the elevator finds 3 and 5 on its way to topFloor 10, and stops there since both are in the requests list. Going down from 10, it finds 5 before 3 because it is farther up, so the second example gives 5 followed by 3. An empty request list gives no stop at all.

