    stops = []
    if not requests:
        return stops
    stopped_at_start = start_floor in requests
    if direction == 1:
        for floor in range(start_floor, top_floor + 1):
            if floor in requests:
                stops.append(floor)
    else:
        for floor in range(start_floor, 0, -1):
            if floor in requests:
                stops.append(floor)
    if stopped_at_start:
        stops.pop(0)
    return stops
```

The task describes a directed traversal with the elevator checking every floor along the way to a limit, including its starting position only if it is explicitly requested. The function builds the list in the direction given.

An inner loop runs from the starting floor to the limit, advancing one floor at a time. Each floor is added to `stops` only if it belongs to the requests list. The loop skips floors that are not in the list, so the sweep is never prematurely broken.

The one case that needs careful treatment is whether the starting floor was itself requested. Because each floor includes the starting floor in its run, a plain loop of this sort would list the start twice if it is requested. That is exactly what the first example needs: starting at floor 1 and moving up, with 1 not present in [3, 5], the elevator stops only at 3 and 5, so the result is [3, 5] rather than [1, 3, 5]. The fallback removes a leading start_floor if it belonged to the list.

A downward sweep, which checks 10, 9, 8 and so on, builds the list in descending order and leaves those numbers in their natural order. The result for the second example is [5, 3]. With no requests the sweeps produce empty lists, as in the third example. A set on the replacement line, or even a membership test on a list, would not have distinguishable performance.

```python
def elevator_sweep_stops(start_floor, direction, requests, top_floor):
    visited = set(requests)
    if direction < 0:
        step = -1
        limit = 1
        for floor in range(start_floor, limit - 1, step):
            if floor in visited:
                yield floor
        return
    if direction > 0:
        step = 1
        limit = top_floor
        for floor in range(start_floor, limit + 1, step):
            if floor in visited:
                yield floor
        return
    raise ValueError(f'invalid direction {direction}')
```

The elevator starts at the requested floor and moves one floor at a time in the given direction, stopping only at floors listed in requests. The list is treated as a set, so if a floor appears more than once it is still visited once, and start_floor itself is accepted only if it appears in the set.

The direction runs the loop in opposite directions: for a downward sweep, from the start floor the first floor below is visited, and the loop ends when floor drops below 1, and an upward sweep runs through all floors up to the top. Each floor in the path is yielded only if it belongs to the set; nothing in the function sorts the requested floors, so their order in the result is the order in which the elevator reaches them.

The empty-requests case is handled naturally, because nothing in the set results in any yield. No check is included for direction values other than 1 and -1, so a caller should never pass a third constant.

```python
def elevator_sweep_stops(start_floor, direction, requests, top_floor):
    stops = []
    for floor in range(start_floor, top_floor + 1, direction):
        if floor in requests:
            stops.append(floor)
        elif floor == 1 and direction == -1:
            break
        elif floor == top_floor and direction == 1:
            stop
        elif floor == 1:
            break
        elif floor == top_floor and direction == -1:
            stop
    return stops
```

The elevator moves one floor at a time in the given direction, stopping at every floor that appears in requests and at start_floor only if it is explicitly requested. It checks each floor in order, so the usual direction loop of a range with step 1 or -1 covers it. It stops accepting stops once it reaches floor 1 (for a downward sweep) or top_floor (for an upward sweep), both at which the final floor is checked before terminating. No reversal is ever needed, because the stops are recorded in the order of the sweep itself.

Notice the special care for the downward sweep reaching floor 1: the terminating floor check for that sweep is on a different expression, namely floor == 1, not on whether floor is at or past the end of the range.

On the third example, start_floor is 1, direction is up and requests is empty, so the elevator never stops and returns an empty list. On the second example, 10 is the start_floor and top_floor with direction down, and [5, 3] is the only requested floor, so the sweep runs from 10 to 1 and picks out 5 and then 3, stopping at 1 after that.

