The function never counts how full the queue is. It keeps a running list of all the arriving persons, and, on each serve event, it simply discards the first n elements, where n is the capacity. After a serve, everything that remains in the list is regarded as still waiting, even though the server can handle capacity people at a time.

This discard-based model agrees with the desideratum only when the capacity never exceeds the number of arriving persons and at most one serve ever happens, because then nothing useful can have been carried over from an earlier serve to a later one.

For example, with a capacity of 1 serving a queue of two, the second person is the one left over and the function returns [2], which is right. A single serve on an empty queue returns [], which is also right. Where it fails is when a serve runs out of people to remove, as in the call [('arrive', 3), ('arrive', 4), ('arrive', 5), ('serve',)] with capacity 2, where persons 3 and 4 are served and 5 remains, so the specification expects [5]. The function keeps the entire list and returns [3, 4, 5]. The third assert, where a serve on an empty queue also returns [], only passes because an empty slice of an empty list is still empty.

